binding.raw_review:
{
  "units": [
    {
      "id": "u1",
      "status": "non_factual",
      "bindings": [],
      "reason": "This is a heading/label for the design phase, not a policy assertion or recommendation."
    },
    {
      "id": "u2",
      "status": "supported",
      "bindings": [
        {
          "number": 2,
          "quote": "Agencies must assess all new AI use cases against the in-scope criteria (Appendix C) to determine if they are in scope of the policy. The assessment must be documented and take place during the design phase while developing requirements."
        },
        {
          "number": 2,
          "quote": "For AI use cases that are in-scope, agencies must conduct an AI use case impact assessment. Agencies are to commence an assessment at the design stage. Before the solution is deployed, agencies must finalise the assessment and apply any agreed risk treatments."
        }
      ],
      "reason": "Child 2 supports the documented in-scope assessment during design and the requirement to commence, finalise and apply risk treatments before deployment."
    },
    {
      "id": "u3",
      "status": "supported",
      "bindings": [
        {
          "number": 1,
          "quote": "Agencies may conduct an AI use case impact assessment by using either: \u2022 the Australian Government AI impact assessment tool (the impact assessment tool) \u2022 an internal process that integrates all provisions of the impact assessment tool."
        },
        {
          "number": 1,
          "quote": "Where an agency integrates the tool, they must ensure: \u2022 the internal process is consistent \u2022 it delivers the same (or a higher) risk outcome for inherent and residual risk. Agencies must be able to revise their internal process in response to any impact assessment tool updates."
        }
      ],
      "reason": "Child 1 supports the choice of tool or integrated internal process, consistency, same-or-higher risk outcome, and ability to revise for tool updates."
    },
    {
      "id": "u4",
      "status": "non_factual",
      "bindings": [],
      "reason": "This is a heading/label for registration and deployment-stage monitoring, not a policy assertion or recommendation."
    },
    {
      "id": "u5",
      "status": "supported",
      "bindings": [
        {
          "number": 1,
          "quote": "Agencies must add each in-scope AI use case to their internal register of AI use cases and update it as required. Include risk rating and accountable use case owner changes."
        },
        {
          "number": 1,
          "quote": "When deploying an in-scope AI use case, agencies must: \u2022 regularly monitor and evaluate their use case to ensure it is operating as intended and that risks are being effectively managed. \u2022 re-validate the AI use case impact assessment by checking its accuracy and updating it when there is a material change in the use case scope, usage or operation."
        }
      ],
      "reason": "Child 1 supports register addition/updates, deployment monitoring/evaluation, and re-validation on material change."
    },
    {
      "id": "u6",
      "status": "supported",
      "bindings": [
        {
          "number": 1,
          "quote": "Agencies should also monitor changes that are not initiated by the agency. For example, vendor changes and changes in the regulatory environment. Agencies could also ask vendors to provide information on updates through contractual mechanisms."
        }
      ],
      "reason": "Child 1 supports monitoring non-agency-initiated changes and asking vendors for update information through contractual mechanisms."
    },
    {
      "id": "u7",
      "status": "non_factual",
      "bindings": [],
      "reason": "This is a heading/label for risk-tiered governance, not a policy assertion or recommendation."
    },
    {
      "id": "u8",
      "status": "supported",
      "bindings": [
        {
          "number": 3,
          "quote": "If an agency determines their in-scope AI use case has an inherent medium-risk rating when completing an AI use case impact assessment, they should consider if the use case would benefit from being governed through a designated board or a senior executive. Agencies should choose an approach appropriate for the size and scope of the agency if they apply additional governance."
        },
        {
          "number": 3,
          "quote": "If an agency determines their in-scope AI use case has an inherent high-risk rating when completing an AI use case impact assessment, they must: \u2022 report the use case to the agency accountable official with the reasons for the inherent high-risk rating, proposed mitigations and residual risks \u2022 govern the use case through a designated board or a senior executive, whichever is appropriate for the size and scope of the agency."
        },
        {
          "number": 3,
          "quote": "Once an agency has decided to deploy the use case, they must: \u2022 report the use case to the DTA through the accountable official, see the Standard for accountability \u2022 establish a system to regularly review the use case every 12 months at a minimum. The review must report to the relevant governing board or senior executive on whether the use case is operating as intended and that risks are being effectively managed. The review must also consider the AI use case impact assessment and revisions to it, if required."
        }
      ],
      "reason": "Child 3 supports medium-risk consideration, high-risk reporting/governance, DTA reporting, and at-least-12-month review requirements."
    },
    {
      "id": "u9",
      "status": "non_factual",
      "bindings": [],
      "reason": "This is a heading/label for out-of-scope use cases and material change, not a policy assertion or recommendation."
    },
    {
      "id": "u10",
      "status": "supported",
      "bindings": [
        {
          "number": 4,
          "quote": "For use cases assessed as out of scope of this policy, agencies may adopt the use case while ensuring they comply with relevant existing obligations, such as privacy and security. If an agency adopts an out-of-scope AI use case, they must assess whether the use case becomes in-scope of this policy if there is a material change in the scope, usage or operation of the solution. If a use case is in scope, agencies must follow any applicable actions in this policy."
        }
      ],
      "reason": "Child 4 supports adoption with existing obligations, reassessment on material change, and following applicable actions for in-scope use cases."
    },
    {
      "id": "u11",
      "status": "non_factual",
      "bindings": [],
      "reason": "This is a heading/label for timing, not a policy assertion or recommendation."
    },
    {
      "id": "u12",
      "status": "supported",
      "bindings": [
        {
          "number": 2,
          "quote": "Agencies must begin AI use case assessments within 12 months of this policy taking effect. For existing use cases not yet assessed, agencies must determine whether they are in scope of this policy and apply all relevant policy actions by 30 April 2027. Where practicable, agencies should implement the requirements ahead of the deadlines listed above."
        },
        {
          "number": 7,
          "quote": "While agencies may need this time to action requirements, agencies should implement them sooner if practicable. Agencies could consider putting in place interim processes and building out their approach as they reach the specified implementation deadline."
        }
      ],
      "reason": "Child 2 supports the 12-month start and 30 April 2027 deadline; Child 7 supports implementing sooner and interim processes."
    },
    {
      "id": "u13",
      "status": "non_factual",
      "bindings": [],
      "reason": "This is a heading/label for evidence gaps, not a policy assertion or recommendation."
    },
    {
      "id": "u14",
      "status": "gap",
      "bindings": [],
      "reason": "The available excerpts do not establish specific medium-risk monitoring cadence or re-validation triggers beyond general deployment monitoring/re-validation, nor internal register content/format beyond risk rating and accountable owner changes."
    }
  ]
}
